// A value `if` whose arms have DIFFERENT integer widths answers with the arm
// that ran.
//
// `if a > 0 { 11 } else { 7i16 }` in an `Int`-returning function compiled, and
// `f(-3)` printed 11 — the THEN arm's value, on the path that took the ELSE
// arm. The identical program with a bare `7` printed 7. Nothing warned, nothing
// panicked, the exit code was 0: a wrong ANSWER, which is the worst thing a
// compiler can produce and the reason this was never merely a diagnostics bug.
//
// RULED (design 195 rule 2): a value-branch arm is a TRANSFER, so it takes the
// rule a `return` takes — a lossless widening is free. `7i16` is an `Int16` and
// `Int16` widens into `Int` with no loss, so the arms merge at `Int` and the
// else path answers 7. This file is the flip its own earlier text named: it
// said "if the ruling instead widens same-sign arms implicitly, this pin
// becomes EXPECT: success printing 11 and 7 — but it can never stay as it is."
//
// What is NOT legal is an arm that cannot widen losslessly: a narrowing arm, or
// a same-width sign flip like `7u64` beside an `Int`. Conformance row W13 holds
// those; W12 holds the acceptance side at every arm order and signedness.
//
// The loud sibling was DF-192f: `n * 2i16` on an `Int n` was a codegen ICE
// rather than a wrong answer. One root — nothing checked that two integer
// operands agree — and design 195 answered both, differently, because an
// OPERATOR has two peers and a BRANCH has one destination.
//
// Found by tools/sawfuzz.py (its literal-rewrite mutation suffixes a bare
// literal); the ICE it reported minimized into this silent version.
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// 11
// 7

func f(a: Int) -> Int {
    if a > 0 { 11 } else { 7i16 }
}

func main() {
    print(f(3))
    print(f(-3))
}
